as the pandemic worsens many americans  are also facing a desperate economic  plight but  congress has been at a stalemate for  months over a relief bill  nancy cordes is on capitol hill nancy  key protections for millions are due to  expire  by the end of the month is there any  progress at all  there is now anthony this bipartisan  group of senators about eight to ten of  them who are trying to revive  negotiations  and come up with some kind of stimulus  deal before the holidays but  i have to tell you it is a very tall  order they would have to figure out a  way to bridge the gap  between the 500 billion dollar package  that senate republicans want  and the 2 trillion dollars that house  democrats have proposed  now on capitol hill today federal  reserve chair jerome powell is going to  testify that the pace of  economic recovery since pandemic  lockdowns  has moderated it has slowed that's why  this is such a critical  debate and treasury secretary steven  mnuchin is also going to testify he's  going to urge congress  to pass a bipartisan bill to distribute  about 450 billion dollars  in unused funds from the march cares act  now a bill that size might include more  funding for business loans and grants  but it probably wouldn't include  another round of stimulus checks or a  boost in unemployment insurance that is  something that is very important  to democrats but on the senate floor  yesterday the republican leader mitch  mcconnell urged democrats  to take what they can get at this point  he argued essentially that half a loaf  is better than no loaf at all tony all  right nancy thank you very much
 

